>
  <h2>Sometimes the house is right — it’s just too small.</h2>
  <p>
    You love your street. The kids are settled in school. You’re not ready to start over with a new
    mortgage and a new neighborhood. The only problem is the house itself: there’s no room for the
    family office, the growing kids need their own spaces, or the primary bedroom feels like an
    afterthought. An addition fixes the one thing that’s wrong without giving up everything that’s
    right.
  </p>
  <p>
    That’s the real question behind most additions — not “how big,” but “is adding on smarter than
    moving?” We’ll help you answer that honestly, even when the honest answer is the smaller job for us.
  </p>
  <h3>Add on, or move? The honest math</h3>
  <p>
    Moving looks simpler than it is. By the time you add agent commissions, closing costs, moving
    expenses, and a bigger mortgage at today’s rates, the “fresh start” gets expensive fast — and you
    still lose the neighborhood, the schools, and the home you’ve already put work into. An addition
    keeps all of that and solves the space problem directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    It doesn’t pencil out for everyone, and we’ll say so when it doesn’t. If your lot is too tight or
    the structure can’t support what you want, we won’t talk you into a project that fights your house.
    But when you love where you live and just need more room, an addition is very often the better call —
    and that’s a conversation we’re happy to have before you ever commit.
  </p>
  <h3>Real square footage means real structure</h3>
  <p>
    This is where an addition is genuinely different from a remodel. You’re not refreshing a space that
    already exists — you’re building a new one that has to tie into the old one and stand on its own.
    That means a proper foundation for a ground-level addition, and for a second story, engineering to
    confirm your existing foundation and framing can carry the new load. We start there, because getting
    the structure right is what keeps the finished addition solid for decades.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second-story addition is the bigger undertaking of the two. It touches the roof, the existing
    walls, and sometimes the foundation, and it needs engineering before anything else. We’ll tell you
    up front whether your home can support one — because the last thing we want is for you to fall in
    love with a plan your house can’t carry.
  </p>
  <h3>It should look like it was always there</h3>
  <p>
    The tell-tale sign of a cheap addition is that you can spot it from the curb — a roofline that
    doesn’t quite line up, siding that’s a shade off, windows that don’t match the rest of the house.
    Making an addition disappear into the original home is most of the craft, and it takes extra care on
    older houses, where materials sometimes have to be matched or sourced. Done right, there’s no seam
    you can point to — it just looks like your home, with more of it.
  </p>
  <h3>Permits, systems, and one team that owns it</h3>
  <p>
    Additions trigger the full permit process: plans, structural calculations, and inspections. We
    manage that so you’re not the one on the phone with the city, and so the work passes without the
    expensive do-overs that come from cutting corners. The new space also needs its systems extended —
    electrical, heating and cooling, and often plumbing — so it performs as well as the rest of the
    house instead of being the room that’s always too hot or too cold.
  </p>
  <h3>What your investment gets you</h3>
  <p>
    Room additions generally start around <strong>$100K</strong>, with second-story additions running
    higher because of the added structural and engineering work. The number comes down to square
    footage, whether it’s ground-level or a second story, and whether the new space includes a bathroom
    or kitchen. After we walk your home and your lot, we’ll give you an honest range for your exact
    project — and a straight answer about whether an addition is really the right move for you.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
